603 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Studies have shown, for instance, that families have resisted mental health treatments that challenge hierarchical structures, and or ask children to talk about personal struggles to one another, so that children could be in turn demanding family harmony or even, upset their elders in such hierarchy. )
/Pg 14 0 R
>>
endobj
604 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
605 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Despite the reported positive correlation between authoritarian filial piety and mental health, there is reason to believe that it will also reinforce harmful behavioral patterns in families where children struggle with mental health conditions. Yeh \(2018\) explains that parental expectations rooted in authoritarian filial piety will delegitimize children's mental health concerns, framing them as a lack of discipline rather than a legitimate medical issue. Furthermore, Kwan et.al, 2021, shows the emphasis on hierarchy and obedience prevents children from openly discussing mental distress, leading to underutilization of mental health services. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
606 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Although Leck \(2023\) found a positive correlation between both reciprocal and authoritarian filial piety and self-reported mental well-being, in Confucian-influenced societies like Singapore, individuals equate adherence to filial duty with virtue or satisfaction, even if this obedience comes at the expense of their own psychological needs. The study also does not sufficiently examine how these dynamics play out in families experiencing severe or chronic mental illness, where the stakes of emotional suppression and caregiver strain are far more acute.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
