/S /P
/E (Heavy financial burdens severely restricted textile manufacturing exports while maximizing profits for the British East India Company and British industrial growth. Indian textiles, specifically exquisite cotton fabrics like muslin and calico, sparked highly unanticipated demand in Europe. Regional demand was inspired by the idiosyncratic local Indian artisanal designs exported from the 1680s. Raw cotton production from India only competed with approximately 30% to 55% with British raw cotton prices between 1710 and 1823 [Fig. 1].  British weavers and textile manufacturers thus struggled in production, in competency against the rich-heavy meritoriousness and cost-effective consumption of Indian cotton textiles and artisanal industries. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
