872 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (During these periods of the Anglo-Egyptian and the 2nd Anglo-Afghan Wars, the British-Indian military was an extremely professional force. This was demonstrated through its disciplined organisation and the high levels of experience in both its officer ranks and enlisted ranks. During the Anglo-Egyptian War, the British-Indian force demonstrated its military might through their professionalism. )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
873 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This paper categorises professionalism under the following four points, in no particular order: Competence, Loyalty, High Ethical Standards and Discipline. The British-Indian force of the Anglo-Egyptian War was a well trained force. The force was made up of veterans of the Second Anglo-Afghan War and the Third Anglo-Burmese War. Following the sepoy mutiny of 1857, the British military was reformed in order to prevent future insurrections. This resulted in soldiers having greater technical skills, resulting in their competency improving with both their weapons and their collaboration. Soldiers were trained to and expected to meet far higher standards, and were paid better. The matter of high pay lends itself well to the next point, loyalty. There were two main factors that resulted in the complete loyalty of Indian troops, financial incentives and the martial races being applied to military recruitment. After the Sepoy Mutiny of 1857, Indian soldiers started receiving competitive pay and benefits. Pay not only served as a motivator but also as a way of securing the loyalty of Indian troops, ensuring that they were financially invested in the success.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
