880 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
881 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The officers and soldiers of the British-Indian army also had extremely high ethical standards. In the officer class, this was shaped by British colonial values and upbringing, and it played a significant role in maintaining discipline and order within the army. They were trained to lead by example, as their leadership would set the bar for the performance of their troops. This was demonstrated in the Anglo-Egyptian War of 1882 by commanders such as General Sir Gerald Graham, who was known for his ethical conduct toward prisoners and personally leading his men into battle. Another example of high ethical standards was the standards of Field Marshal Garnet Joseph Wolseley, who maintained strict orders against looting and unnecessary destruction of Cairo and neighbouring cities. Finally, discipline was exhibited by both officers and soldiers of the British-Indian force. There was a strict chain of command, with officers being directly responsible for the actions of their soldiers. In the specific example of the Anglo-Egyptian War, Sir Garnet Woseley was known for maintaining a force known as the 'Wolseley Ring', a group of officers he entrusted with the responsibility of, among others, maintaining discipline and order within the force. The soldiers of the British-Indian army were part of an army wherein discipline was enforced through strict training, clear orders, and punishment for misconduct. Soldiers demonstrated this discipline by maintaining strict battlefield discipline, remaining calm and in formation under fire. The strict discipline of the British-Indian army was one of the key reasons that they were able to defeat a force that severely outnumbered them. Therefore, during this period, the British-Indian army was an extremely professional force, exhibiting discipline, high ethical standards, loyalty, and competence.  )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
882 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
883 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (From the beginning of the first world war to when India gained her independence on 15th August 1947, the British-Indian military transformed significantly. Originally established as a force to defend the interests of the British Empire, it began to perform roles that are traditionally associated with internal governance organisations. While it still fought in foreign theatres, it was also tasked with maintaining stability within the British-Indian Empire. This was crucial due to increased demand for self-rule within India. )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
