/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In the years following the First World War, the Indian Military was mainly involved in suppressing discontent with the British administration. Within the military, reactions to this were mixed. The reactions to the role of the military as a constabulary force varied based on their backgrounds and positions. Many Indian soldiers felt tension between their sense of duty to the British and the nationalistic sentiments that were beginning to grow within the Indian population. Indian officers within the military experienced similar conflict. Nonetheless, a combination of their professionalism and fear of punishment kept soldiers from openly defying orders. The reformation of the military following the 1857 mutiny was another key factor in the maintenance of order and discipline within the British-Indian army, as recruitment policies ensured that soldiers believed they were different from the rest of the Indian community. For British officers though, particularly the senior officers, this role as a constabulary force was viewed as a necessary part of maintaining control. While some British officers were uncomfortable with the methods employed during incidents such as the Jallianwala Bagh Massacre, they were extremely unlikely to refuse orders as a result of their training. As the 1930s progressed, the military's role as a constabulary force was questioned by both the Indian public and some members within the military. The Indian army saw growing unrest, particularly with the harshness of British responses to civil disobedience. This unrest was reflected in events such as the 1946 Royal Indian Navy Mutiny. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
891 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (During this period, the army was frequently deployed to quell protests, strikes, and public unrest. Their methods often involved violent suppression, using live ammunition, lethal weapons, and in extreme cases, machine guns to disperse crowds gathered to protest British rule.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
892 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
893 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (While the British-Indian Military played the role of a constabulary force from 1919-1939, it also was a traditional combat force, playing the role that it was established to do, which was to protect and expand British colonial interests. The British-Indian military saw its primary role as safeguarding British interests overseas and projecting British power across the globe. Although its effort was equally divided, the soldiers were not trained to serve as a constabulary force or act as policemen; instead, they were trained specifically for external combat. While many soldiers, especially those of Indian descent, felt conflicted about their role as a constabulary force within India, the soldiers and officers of the British-Indian military viewed deployment as an external force as an obligation. Between the start of World War 1 and India's independence, the army participated in numerous campaigns that were part of the broader British imperial strategy. For the soldiers, especially those of the martial races, military service was often seen as an honorable means of gaining recognition and respect. The British-Indian military fought alongside British forces in Europe, Africa, and the Middle East, including in key battles such as the battle in Mesopotamia during World War I and in North Africa during World War II. During these campaigns, soldiers of the British-Indian army were awarded scores of medals for valour and gallantry. In addition, they remained a professional force, demonstrating high levels of competence, ethical standards, loyalty, and discipline. This professionalism was vital in maintaining the effectiveness of the military during periods of pressure, such as both the world wars and subsequent internal unrest within India. This capacity to be effective during periods of stress combined with the professionalism of the military ensured the British-Indian military remained largely unaffected by the rising nationalist movements of the 1920s and 1930s. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
