939 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This became evident during the First Kashmir War of 1947-48. Building on research done by Chari and Mukherjee, this paper believes that the pattern of civil military relations in India \(as argued by Chari and Mukherjee\) reflects the prioritization of civilian control over the military. While military commanders played a crucial role in both strategic and operational details, areas which civilian leaders lacked expertise, the ultimate decisions always lay in the hands of civilian leaders. During the Kashmir War, the final decisions were always made by Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru, Deputy Prime Minister Vallabhbhai Patel, and VP Menon. )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
940 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Examples of these decisions include the request for international intervention \(considered by both Dinkar P. Srivastava [find pg number, left library before realising and Rathnam Indurthy [find pg number, left library before realising] in their respective books as the most significant decision taken during the Kashmir War\) and the deployment of forces. )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
941 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (According to both Srivastava and Indurthy, the request for international intervention was not only sought to bring a ceasefire but also a way to internationalize the Kashmir issue, placing it under global scrutiny and paving the way for future negotiations. This was despite the military's preference for continued engagement. The decision to deploy forces was also a decision taken by civilian leadership, as it was taken by Nehru and Patel on the advice of Lord Mountbatten in his role as Governor-General of India and VP Menon. The decision to deploy troops in Kashmir was done only after the Maharaja had signed the instrument of accession, which was taken against the advice of senior military leaders. )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
942 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
943 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Overall, the Kashmir War was more than a military conflict, instead, it was a significant period in the formation of India as a state. It shaped the course of Indian and international history and placed India on the world stage by proving both its democratic capabilities and its military might. )
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
