1014 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (in ancient Greek culture. While existing scholarship provides valuable insights into divine )
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1015 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (interventions and gender roles, several gaps remain. Further research is needed to explore the circumstances and roles of marginalized women in these myths and the differences between intervention for male and female mortals, comparing them to their interventions with female gods and male gods respectively. In several pieces of research, only one myth is examined or only one gender, mortal or god is taken into account. By comparing and contrasting all of them at the same time, while using multiple different evidence of myths, I believe we are able to get a more detailed and revealing answer to the extent of how Greek myths altered societal norms. Additionally, a more nuanced understanding of agency and motivations of male and female deities is warranted, moving beyond simplistic portrayals of myths that reinforce them as either enforcers or victims of the patriarchy. )
/Pg 53 0 R
/K [11]
>>
