1028 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (I will attempt to answer the research question by examining the differences between the extent and expectations in divine intervention between female and male gods, and female and male mortals. To reveal extreme similarities and differences from which we can infer how these myths influenced Greek cultural attitudes towards women during the classical period. To answer the research question, I have split my research into four distinct criterias. Male gods and their interactions with male mortals, as well as their interactions with female mortals. Female gods and their interactions with female mortals, as well as male mortals. I believe this method cross examines all aspects deeply and is a more thorough investigation of the mythologies compared to other studies. By comparing at least ten different myths in each section I am going to point out key differences in circumstances, expectations and ideologies in all areas of Greek mythology. By listing them all out and then finding the exact circumstance and explanation for behaviour for each and every myth, I gain a deeper understanding of the true meaning the myth was trying to convey and how society may have interpreted these myths during the classical period. )
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1029 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The significance of this question is not to be underestimated as these myths have stood the test of time, and have been passed down through generations of warriors for a reason. By understanding and comparing the different interactions between men and women, and gods and goddesses, we can infer further details about women's life in Greece and how these myths might have influenced or perpetuated societal ideology and standards. The simple resilience of these norms suggests that they have been a crucial part of Greek and Western culture as these timeless themes hold symbolic depth, which is why they continue to shape language, literature, art, and societal values across generations, serving as foundational narratives that inform both individual identity and collective heritage in the Western world.)
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
