<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The second noteworthy pattern is the reaction of the female god to the female mortal portrayed in these myths. Instead of questioning or punishing the male god or mortal who is almost one hundred percent of the cases primarily at fault, they turn to their own kind, female mortals as the root cause of the problem. This results in a physical or mental disfigurement on the mortal women which means the women suffer immense pain and loss, when it usually should not be theirs in the first place. The third pattern would be the entire irony of the situation and what that translates to in real life. The entirety of these myths could be summed up to Zeus cheating on Hera multiple times and yet the poor mortal woman gets punished harshly as if it were entirely her fault. These myths being perpetuated could subconsciously reinforce society's standards that if a man cheats on a woman it could be forgiven easily, as it is so easy to fall to charms as a man. Yet if a woman does so just once, there are incredible repercussions and consequences, and society would forget there was even another man in the picture with whom she cheated with. The blame would fall entirely on her as she should uphold her chastity and never give in to such whims, until marriage, which she is not allowed to choose either and is instead chosen by her father or brother. )
