1055 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (While analysing the unfortunate circumstances between male gods and female mortals, I will be highlighting the fact that more often than not, the interactions with mortal women often purely involve seduction, abduction and imposition, reflecting a cultural acceptance of male dominance and the objectification of women. The contrasting portrayal of divine-mortal relationships with male gods often engaging in fleeting, often coercive or forceful encounters that result in the suffering of mortal women, versus the rarity of sustained, mutually beneficial relationships involving female deities and mortal men reflects a cultural anxiety surrounding female power and sexual agency, reinforces the modern trope that powerful women cannot sustain relationships with lesser men as their desires are seen as inherently destabilizing. While examining these myths, a repeated pattern of carelessness and lack of respect will be discovered. This exposes the mentality of mortal and divine men and vulnerability of mortal women to divine power and the societal acceptance of male entitlement over female bodies and destinies. This is in relation to my broader argument about how myths set the stage for cultural norms, and we can begin to truly understand this by seeing the circumstances and poor treatment of women in these myths. )
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [7]
>>
