1074 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Again, Daphne and Apollo. The god physically chases after her multiple times until she must resort to Zeus to save her from this fate. Out of pity, he turns her into a laurel tree to preserve her chastity. The main reason Zeus headed her request was because it would keep her virginity, and Apollo was purely after her beauty. Poseidon initially pursued Amphitrite, a beautiful sea nymph, to be his wife. But after multiple rejections to protect her virginity, he coerced her into becoming his wife. The most saddening myth of Apollo and Cassandra. Apollo bestowed the gift of prophecy to seduce her, but Cassandra rejected him. Enraged, he cursed her so that nobody would believe her prophecies. If he had not committed such a callous act out of spite, the fall of Troy could have been prevented. The trojan horse, the war, and millions of deaths may have been avoided, if only the gods could handle mere rejection. A very interesting pattern occurs that women only fleed in instances where their chastity and virginity must be protected. It is a mixture of fear of the god and what might happen to them, and the societal pressure that they must preserve their virginity that makes them flee improper circumstances. And those who enforced those societal standards, the gods, are the very ones taking the mortal women against their will and doing as they please. If the women repudiate the male gods, they risk the entirety of the gods unfair wrath. In the end, the women are never faced with the freedom of choice. These myths directly correlate to society as men and kings in real life would have multiple wives and never treat women as equal. This would be accepted because the myths that the people learnt as their only sources of entertainment, would women like so. )
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1075 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (It is notable that Zeus, the god associated with marriage, is responsible for the majority of infidelities involving male gods and mortal women. This highlights a significant power imbalance within the mythological framework, where male gods act with little accountability while female mortals bear the consequences. The repeated punishment of women, despite their often being victims of male aggression, reflects and reinforces the patriarchal values of classical society, normalizing the control and subjugation of female agency. Tales of male gods and mortal women reveal a consistent pattern of male impunity and female punishment, epitomized by Zeus's own infidelities. This dynamic underscores a profound power imbalance that served to reinforce patriarchal norms within classical society, where male dominance was normalized and female agency was suppressed. )
