1100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Lastly, to fully deepen our understanding of myths, expectations and attitudes we must examine the expectations and circumstances between male gods and male mortals, which emphasize themes of mentorship, legacy and the validation of their egos through acts of heroism. The contrast between the glorified and often heroic circumstances of mortal men compared to the horrific, rather desperate circumstances of mortal women in the event of divine intervention reflects a cultural devaluation of female well-being and a prioritisation of male achievement. While analysing these myths, a pattern of paternal support and patronage. The mortal men are often part of, or venturing on a quest in which the male gods are aiding them in. In both male god and female goddesses intervention regarding male mortals, the primary circumstance occuring is guidance and gifting. Compared to the intervention with female mortals which differ drastically, often stripping away their basic rights. These narratives underscore the importance of male lineage, the pursuit of glory, and the role of divine approval in achieving lasting fame, revealing a culture forced on perpetuating male dominance. This is in relation to my broader argument of divine intervention for men and women in myths and how this reveals cultural attitudes towards women in Greece. By examining the interactions between male gods and male mortals, we can connect the final link between myths and how they perpetuated stereotypes that regulated women for centuries.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
