/S /P
/E (It is the factors above that all contributed to the unification of the religious populace up until 651 CE with the annexation of Persia. We must analyze the theological similarities above and the strings that weaved together every Zoroastrians day to day life in order for us to truly understand the magnitude of disruption caused by Arab occupation of Persia.)
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Before analyzing and decrypting the relationship between Zoroastrianism and Islam within Persia leading up to the annexation of Persia, we must first understand the influence Zoroastrianism had on the creation of Islam and all Abrahamic faiths. This crucial analysis also allows us to further prove why exactly was Zoroastrianism susceptible to division post-annexation.)
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The reason historians know about the correlation between Zoroastrianism and the main Abrahamic faiths \(Christianity, Judaism, Islam\) is because of the idea of dualism. Zoroastrianism being debatedly the oldest religion in the world is bound to have some sort of influence over religions to come, but the magnitude of influence was more significant than one would assume. The core principle of Zoroastrianism as mentioned in the introduction is the idea of good versus evil. Ahura Mazda the omnipotent god created two twins Spenta Mainyu \(force of good\) and Angra Mainyu/Ahriman \(force of evil\) the battle for influence over the world between these two twins is the Zoroastrian explanation for all the events of the world. Not only is this general idea of good vs evil, portrayed in all Abrahamic faiths but even the scriptural references are extremely similar; Both Zoroastrianism and the Abrahamic faiths mention the battle of light versus darkness. In Chapters 40-48 of the Book of Isaiah, there are extreme resemblances with the third and fourth verses of the Gatha 44. This further proves the idea that the susceptibility to the division of Zoroastrianism was no fault of theological ideology but instead, over-integration with the region.)
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
