>>
endobj
1183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (It is important to directly analyze the interactions of Islam and Zoroastrianism pre-annexation to understand not only what the possible motives of the Arabs were but also to establish the basis for similarities between the two religions. It is important to note here that Islam was the only other prominent religion near Persia at the time and therefore it is speculated that the Arabs could have been competing for land, buy in to their religion, or resources. It was part of the Islamic scripture to spread the word of Allah contrary to Zoroastrianism scripture where conversion was more frowned upon as it was a symbol of impurity, there is consensus among the academic community that this was one of if not the the largest motive for invasion. Considering the hostility between the groups demonstrated by the invasion, one would imagine there was no significant direct interaction between the Zoroastrians and the Arabs, nevertheless there were semblances of interaction between the two nations such as trade conducted by the Sassanian Zoroastrians with Arabic tribes through the trade routes in the Persian Gulf. This trade is theorized as the means by which Zoroastrianism was able to influence Islam, it was said that on trade excursions Zoroastrian traders would introduce the idea of dualism as well as the purity of fire something that we can see being prominent in Islamic scripture. We can analyze through Islam Mahdi\(translation-savior\) and Zoroastrians Saoshynant\(translation: bringer of light\) that there is a significant overlap in ideology inside of the two scriptures. Both discuss a savior who will appear at the end of time to restore humanity and make the world pure. Another similarity is the significance of fire and light as symbols of purity in Zoroastrianism as well as the depiction of Allah in Islam as the "Light of the heavens and the earth" \(surah:24.35\). Through this, we can conclude that while not substantial there was certainly interaction between Zoroastrianism and Islam pre-Islamic expansion.)
/Pg 69 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (To evaluate some of the sources that present evidence of Zoroastrian influence over biblical religions. Sections Daniel 1:21,6:28 and 10:1 and Ezra 1:1 and 6:14 as well as Isaiah 44:24,44:25,44:26,44:37,44:28 and 45:1. All speak positively about the Zoroastrian influence over the biblical religion. It is important to analyze though that there is possible bias here as this may simply be an attempt at claiming more interaction with the Persian region and claims to land in the area something the biblical population has made evident as a goal. It is important to note as well that the lack of Zoroastrian records from the Achaemenid period and the lack of documentation of early Biblical and Islamic scripture does serve as a barrier to large-scale analysis on the scale of influence of Zoroastrianism on the Abrahamic faiths.)
/Pg 69 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Effectively what we have proven through direct analysis of religious scripture is that there are noticeable Zoroastrian theological overlaps with the Abrahamic faiths likely as an impact of trade relations in the region. This means that it was not the theological aspect of the religion that led to division and cannot be considered the main reason for Zoroastrianism's susceptibility to division. )
/Pg 69 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Finally, before moving on to the post-occupational analysis of the two parties, we must analyze and look at the pre-conquest unity and institutionalism the Zoroastrians relied on. This is the precursor to analyzing why the Zoroastrians were not able to remain unified post-displacement of parts of the population. This directly proves why Zoroastrianism was susceptible to division post-annexation.)
/Pg 69 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
