1194 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The earliest point to which we can date Zoroastrianism as being prevalent/dominant in Persia is the Achaemenid Empire. Under the rule of Cyrus the Great and then Darius the Great, the Zoroastrian religion was institutionalized within Persia an example of this is Zoroastrian law being the law that all of Persia operated on. Whilst there is debate as to whether or not Cyrus the Great was Zoroastrian we can likely analyze that the Persian population definitely was predominantly Zoroastrian during his rule. This is for two reasons, the first being that the rule of Cyrus the Great and Darius the Great was only ten years apart, for there to be such a religious variance between a population not being Zoroastrian to a ruler who would come only ten years later to publicly praise and revere Ahura Mazda, would be nearly impossible, due to the lack of state-wide infrastructure for communtication at the time2 the only way to spread the word of Zarathustra would be through word of mouth, it is extremely unlikely word of mouth alone was able to spread through the whole Persian empire in just ten years. Along with this, we know for a fact that Darius the Great was Zoroastrian and revered Zoroastrianism this knowledge is accrued through the archaeological evidence we can gather from the time.)
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1195 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 507 0 R
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The above is a photo of the Behestun inscriptions in Iran, A archaeologists date it towards the Sassanian empire more specifically the rule of Darius the great. We can analyze from the photo that there is a group of people around a spiritual figure in the air. We know that this figure is the symbol of Ahura Mazda the omnipotent god in Zoroastrianism, as this same symbol was used multiple times after the behestun inscriptions in scriptures like the Zend Avesta. The general academic consensus around the inscriptions is that the people being rounded up with their hands behind their backs on the right side of the frame are people who have committed some sort of crime and the person who appears to be largest towards the left of the screen is Darius the great holding them for judgment with Ahura Mazda above him. If the rhetoric that Darius the Great spreading through inscriptions was that you would be judged according to the principles of Zoroastrianism it is quite likely that the population was Zoroastrian, otherwise, there would be revolt and discontent among the population for forcing them to live their lives off the principles of religion they don't believe in, yet we have no evidence of such discontent. Following the rule of Darius the Great, the Persian identity became centered around Zoroastrian principles such as worship of fire and Magi priesthood, this furthers the idea that the over-unification of Zoroastrianism with Persian culture was the reason for the divide post-annexation. This analysis proves that it is likely that Cyrus the Great was indeed Zoroastrian, and helps us further understand the scale of which Zoroastrianism was accepted in the early stages of the Persian empire.)
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
