1204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Moving on to the Parthian empire the Zoroastrian identity was still very strongly linked with the Persian identity, More fire temples were built and the reciting of the Yasna became an everyday practice. even though there was Greek influence in Persia in this period the religion stayed relatively resilient towards this influence and continued down the streamlined path of expansion through the region. )
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The early stages of the Sassanian empire can be seen as the peak of the Zoroastrian religion, Magi priesthood was common, fire temples were being built often and Zoroastrianism became the official state-sanctioned religion. This official period of institutionalization by recognizing Zoroastrianism as the state religion and centering laws around it re-confirms the idea of Persians' identity being infused with Zoroastrianism. The issue with creating this uniform identity although, was that every person had their own place of the spectrum of self-association to their identity, some would value the Persian part of this identity more than the Zoroastrian part, others the opposite. This semi-forceful unification of their identity led to an ununified decision calculus when the decision to leave or stay had to be made.)
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Come 651 CE we finally arrived at the annexation of Persia by the Arabs led by caliph Umar, notable battles had been lost by the Persians previously such as the battle of Nahavand in 642 CE that had led to the moment where King Yazdegerd III leader of the Sassanian empire was murdered marking the end of the war and an Arab victory. After the occupation the Ummayads \(the first Arab dynasty\) wasted no time conducting rapid Islamization of the Persian people, considering this was the first Muslim dynasty we can theorize that the Ummayads wanted to spread their religion and ideology as quickly as possible to grow a large base for their future expansions. Within the Persian populace, there were groups of people who converted out of fear for their lives and groups of people who decided to attempt to maintain their religious identity. The problem for the group that chose to maintain its religious identity was that they chose to do this in different ways. Through the Qissa-i-Sanjana we know there were large groups who fled to Sanjan,Gujarat in India they were the Parsis, there were also those who felt conflicted enough about leaving Persia that they decided to stay and developed enclaves in Persia such as Yazd where the largest Zoroastrian fire temple in Iran is located.)
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (When comparing Zoroastrian and Islamic accounts of this event the biases are blatantly present. The Zoroastrians speak of unity through invasion which we know is not true as there is division today as a result of lack of unity, The Arabs glorify the invasion and portray themselves as moral but by ethical standards of today and the past the practices conducted during the annexation were blatantly unethical. This leads us to have to look at facts both parties agree upon to gain an objective account of the events that unfolded.)
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Through the analysis of the unity of the Zoroastrian identity followed by the near-instant schism of the religion it is easily denoted that the occupation of Persia was the driving factor for the longstanding division between the two groups today, it has also been proven the reason for susceptibility to division as not having a unified decision making calculus between the populace. This directly answers what made Zoroastrianism susceptible to division and will become crucial during the second section of the paper. The second section of this paper will move to analyze the direct impacts of division and how it has permeated physically speaking such as cultural division, economic disparity, and societal acceptance.)
/Pg 71 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
