1216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (THE DIVERGENCE)
/E (THE DIVERGENCE)
/Pg 72 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Decisions have consequences. The ancestors of the modern-day Iranian Zoroastrians made the decision to prioritize their loyalty to their state by choosing not to migrate away from the state whilst also choosing not to convert to Islam. Those actions today have practical effects on their genetic descendants. Those who chose to flee their birthplace and migrate to India out of fear for possible religious persecution or forced conversion made a decision as well. That decision has very real consequences not only for their descendants but also for everyone who has ever interacted with them. The goal of this section is to explicitly analyze and decompose what these subtle but also blatant consequences are for each community within zoroastrianism.  )
/Pg 72 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (It is fundamental to establish the consequences of each decision before comparing the two societies to understand what differences are entrenched within them as a result of these decisions. This directly answers the question of what differences there are between the two societies and whether or not they are a result of the Zoroastrian subjugation. The section will continue to be formatted as such, first a dissection of societal differences between the Iranian Zoroastrians and the Parsees, followed by Economic differences and finally Cultural differences.)
/Pg 72 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (To start our dissection of the societal differences present between both societies, lets set some context. After the Arab Invasion in 651CE, the Islamic rulers instituted the jizya tax, a policy that, while first used in Persia, became the standard for all Islamic dominions. The jizya tax was seen as a symbolic transactional relationship where any non-Muslims living in an Islamic state would pay heavy taxes to the government in return for indefinite protection against any form of invasion. One Islamic author named Muhammad Al-tabari wrote work describing the forms of social marginalization taking place within Persia during Islamic rule, he wrote that non-muslim people were not allowed to practice religion publicly and were even not allowed to have certain roles within society. To synthesize the Iranian Zoroastrians, life was not one of religious freedom and autonomy and more closely resonated with domineering oppression.)
/Pg 72 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
