1230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Another pivotal aspect of a religious society is the freedom to practice their religion. In Iran, the Islamic rule destroyed majority of fire temples making it near impossible to practice daily rituals such as the Yasna. This, paired along with the fear of being exposed as a Zoroastrian was a driving factor for the private practice of religion. Private practice entailed families conducting prayers inside their own home and possibly having quite different rituals to other families in the same area. Therefore despite the fact that the Iranian Zoroastrians lived in religious enclaves with each other interacting with each other every day belike, their interpretation and practice of the religion was fragmented as it could only be conducted behind closed doors.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Comparatively, the Parsees in India were more unified in religious practice. Although they were segregated from the rest of society they were not forbade from practising the religion, thus fire temples were established where people would pray together and religious bodies such as Pachayats were established to set rules around how to practice the religion. These factors combined resulted in the societal unification of the parsees and how they practiced religion. A prominent example of this is the Atash Berams \(fire temples\) that were developed in the early years of the parsee residence in india, the most prominent being the Iranshah a large fire temple in Udvada,Gujurat in India. This opportunity for open practice of the religion in a public space supports the premise that the parsees were more and are still more socially unified.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
