/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Anagalously in India, as per the 2011 census, the population of Zoroastrians is 60,000. It is also densely populated in urban areas but almost completely in just one city, Mumbai, Maharashtra. These numbers also reflect a decline that is not as steep as in Iran but still prevalent. Contrarily, in this situation, it cannot be a result of discrimination from the greater society, as parsees are widely accepted. The prime minister of India, Narendra Modhi, often refers to the Parsees in press conferences and speeches, even to the extent that he publicly wished the Parsees a happy Nowruz \(new year as per the Iranian calendar\). This leaves the question as to why there is a population decline in India for the Parsees. The answer is multi-faceted. First of all, the population of Parsees holds uniquely conservative views compared to Zoroastrians globally. They alienate certain groups from joining the religion in multiple ways; for example, children can only join the religion if their father is a parsee and not if their mother is, people from the LGBTQ community are ostracized, and conversion is deeply forbidden. This, as well as the fact that people are moving away from the endogamica that the religion demands, has resulted in a steady population decline for the Parsees over the years.)
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (To synthesize, from a distant lens there are many similarities between the Parsees of India and the Iranian Zoroastrians, commonalities such as that they have formed enclaves or that they have declining populations, but when examining closer, the differences exhibit themselves. Such as the oppressive state that Iranian Zoroastrians live under compared to the accepting integration of the Parsees in India or the conservative views of the Parsees versus the more accepting views of the Iranians. The environment that these two religious groups are set in are vastly different, yet have managed great influence on both the groups resulting in different every day practices and different values.)
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The second subsection under the second section of this paper aims to extensively examine the differences between the Parsees and the Iranian Zoroastrians from an economic standpoint. This sub-section will go over economic differences directly post the Arab conquest of Persia until the modern day, examining what exactly the difference is and how it is a result of the Arab invasion.)
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The nationalist and patriotic nature of the Islamic population resulted in a culture such that people were reluctant to accept other religions, whether the government excepted them or not, many people would have lost husbands, brothers or sons fighting in the Arab Conquest only years before so they would not have a great perception about the Zoroastrians whos army killed said husbands, brothers and sons. )
/Pg 74 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
