1267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The third subsection under the second section of this paper will cover the cultural and demographical differences between the two groups. The cultural differences between the Parsees and the Iranian Zoroastrians have influenced how both groups practice and preach religion from the seventh century AD up until modern day. A synthesis of both groups here is that Iranian Zoroastrians preserved traditions to a further extent due to forced insularity whilst the Parsees not extensively deviating did incorporate aspects of indian culture into their practice of the religion and forsook traditionally Persian aspects. This subsection will elaborate on ritual practices in the two groups in modern and past society, following this the subsection will contain a comparison of the festive differences between the two groups and will conclude with a synthesis of ideas as well as an explanation of how these differences were likely impacted through the Parsee and Iranian Zoroastrian divergence. The cultural and demographic changes between the two groups are a key indicator of the long-term impact of assimilation versus insularity in terms of religious adherence.)
/Pg 77 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Whilst still having the religious freedom to practice, the Iranian Zoroastrians had many barriers stopping them from the open practice of religion. As mentioned in the subsection one on societal differences, there was an open culture of oppression against non-muslims in Iran, limiting Iranian Zoroastrians' practice of religion to private spaces. As mentioned under the Theological section of this paper, the Yasna ceremony is a staple of Zoroastrian Culture and a reminder of Zoroastrian principles as it is performed every day. Iranian Zoroastrians migrated to performing the ceremony inside households, allowing for differences to be made within the ceremony in each household. At the same time the perseverance demonstrated by the Iranian Zoroastrians by continuinign to practice their religioun behind closed doors can be looked at as evidence for why they have had such strong cultural and religious preservation over the years despite oppression.)
/Pg 77 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1269 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Concurrently in India at the time, Religious practice in public was the societal norm. In Gujarat during the 7th-10th century, Chaulukya rulers openly advocated for and continuously tolerated religious freedom. The yasna was performed in newly built firetemples in open spaces for the community to congregate and practice religion daily together the most popular location for this at the time was the Sanjan Atash beram. This was previously unheard of in Persia during the unilateral residence of all zoroastrians, as large congregations were simply not feasible since most atash berams were made to fit a town or village and not more. It was the Hindu influence that created this idea of large religious gatherings, it was the residing Hindus in gujarat that frequently had large religious ceremonies out in the open which influenced not only the way that the Parsees practiced religion in early years but still to this day. The factor of economic stability from trade also helped the Parsees build larger temples and place them in a far more saturated fashion then could be accomplished in Yazd and Kerman. These were the first few cultural adaptations that ended up shaping the Parsee identity that while not Indian and not Persian Zoroastrian fused the two. )
/Pg 77 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
