1277 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (These larger more open daily rituals also created a new standard for festivals and celebrations in the culture. The largest celebration at the time and presently is Nowruz \(Persian New Year\), the most important festival of all in the Persian Calendar, happening at least once a year and sometimes twice it brought together whole communities to celebrate this occasion. Due to the fact that it brought together such large communities meant that it could and did set the standard for culture in communities, which makes it a valuable piece of evidence to analyze for disparities between the Iranian Zoroastrians and the Parsees. The first disparity here is the frequency of celebration. The Iranian Zoroastrians Nowruz celebration overlaps with the Parsees jamshedi Navroz celebration both happening at the spring equinox based on the Falsi calendar. the Parsees also celebrate a second Navroz called Shahanshahi Navroz during August. The Shahanshahi calendar is the Zoroastrian calendar whilst the Falsi calendar is the Persian calendar. The shahashahi calendar was less commonly used in Persia and Parsees adopted it in the process of migration from Persia to India.It is clear here that the Iranian Zoroastrians hold close ties to their persian history and are more reluctant to adapt and change culture as the Parsees have by adopting a secondary calendar. The second difference is the way in which Nowruz/Navroz is practiced, whilst in Iran the zoroastrians see it as a chance to reconnect with god and spend their day in prayer in small groups at local atash berams. The zoroastrians in India have perceived the celebration differently, there is a lesser focus on prayer and a stronger focus on welcoming and celebrating the new year through singing, dancing eating big dinners together in large groups. This is a longer term impact of the Hindu influence on tradition with large ceremonies that associate with religion.)
/Pg 78 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 78 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Analogoulsy in India numbers are larger overall but are declining through the years. The 2001 indian census reported 76,000 Zoroastrians whilst the 2011 census only reported 57,000 a decline of 17% over just ten years. There is unlikely to be any underreporting here because of the religious freedoms of the Parsees explored in subsection 1 as well as the economic benefits of being Parsee in subsection 2. This population decline instead stems from the issue of endogamy. The cultural assimilation of the parsees in india in the modern era, has led to inter-faith marriages becoming increasingly popular. Considering the fact that the governing body of Parsees in india the Bombay Parsee Panchayat has rules regarding whether a child of inter-faith marriage can be a Parsee restricts children from joining at an early age. Only if the childs father is a Zoroastrian can a child be accepted into the community. Whilst the openness of Indian society has provided the parsees with mobility it has at the same time opened them up to cultural assimilation possibly transforming to cultural erosion.)
/Pg 78 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
