<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The broader implication of this study impact the religious understanding of post-conquestial religions. This research can help religions navigate the possible challenges that present themselves when migrating portions of their population to a new location. This research also has current implications to the Zoroastrian community providing an overview of challenges faced in the community a baseline level of understanding needed to make change in the long term to improve longevity of the religion and possibly ensure demographical stability.)
/Pg 79 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1291 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (As with all research this paper does face a certain level of limitation. The possible biases of sources such as the Qissa i sanjan, which is consistently referred to during the paper, mackes it arduous to have an objective overview of historical settings and situations the Parsees were in. The possible fear to identify as Zoroastrian in Iran is another limitation of the sources referred to for demographical analysis. Whilst these are the best possible sources available for analysis at the moment of publication they cannot be considered fully objective or truthful. consequently this paper should not be looked at as an absolute fact, and instead simply the best possible overview of the Zoroastrian divergence between two communities.)
/Pg 79 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
